# Workspace Integration Plugin System

Provider-neutral integration platform for multi-tenant workspaces. Telegram is the first provider plugin; Slack, WhatsApp, Discord, and Email follow the same contract.

{% callout type="note" title="Field exposure migration" %}
Integration delivery field selection is no longer integration-owned. Phase 7 retired
`IntegrationDeliveryIntent` and the `deliveryCandidateFields` response alias; native
`ExposureIntent` is the field-selection store. Phase 8 moves standalone exposure settings to
the native exposure catalog API (`/workspaces/:workspaceId/exposure/catalog`) while
integration transport remains plugin-owned. Workspace **exposure surface defaults** are declared on
`WorkspacePlugin.exposureSurface` — see
[workspace-exposure-plugin-contract.mdoc](./workspace-exposure-plugin-contract.mdoc).
integrations continue to own transport, credentials, routing, and provider formatting only.
Integrations do not own field catalogs.
Domain language (glossary, ADRs, forbidden vocabulary): [Phase 1 closure](../architecture/field-exposure-system.md#phase-1--domain-language-closure). Enforced by `pnpm run guard:field-exposure-phase-0`, `pnpm run guard:field-exposure-phase-1`, and `pnpm run guard:field-exposure-phase-8`.
{% /callout %}

## Goals

- Each workspace (tenant) enables integrations independently.
- Domain modules never call external providers directly.
- Integrations are event-driven via the existing outbox relay.
- Provider adapters own outbound HTTP only (through egress guard).
- Backward compatible with `workspace_telegram_bots` during migration.

## Boundaries

| Layer                                     | Responsibility                                                  |
| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Domain (`tours`, `bookings`, `canonical`) | Persist aggregates; enqueue domain events in outbox TX          |
| Outbox relay                              | Publish in-process bus; run workspace side effects (unchanged)  |
| Integration dispatcher                    | Map published domain events → `integration_delivery_jobs` rows  |
| Integration worker                        | Claim jobs; invoke provider adapters asynchronously             |
| Provider adapter (`telegram`, …)          | API mapping + HTTP only — no business rules                     |
| Workspace plugin `integrationSurface`     | Declares providers, defaults, event mappings, message templates |

## Event flow

```text
HTTP → Service → Prisma TX → outbox_events (pending)
                              ↓
                    outbox relay (unchanged)
                              ↓
              publishDomainEvent (platform-events bus)
                              ↓
         dispatchTourCreatedOutboxSideEffects (workspace bindings — unchanged)
                              ↓
         dispatchIntegrationDomainEvent (NEW — feature-flagged)
                              ↓
              integration_delivery_jobs (pending)
                              ↓
         integration delivery worker (separate poll loop)
                              ↓
              TelegramProviderAdapter.sendMessage
```

**Rule:** Outbox relay must not await provider HTTP. It only inserts delivery jobs.

## Core abstractions

- `IntegrationProviderId` — registry key (`telegram`, `slack`, …).
- `IntegrationCapability` — `message.send`, `channel.create`, `message.delete`, `group.manage`.
- `IntegrationConnection` — tenant-scoped enabled provider + config + credentials reference.
- `IntegrationEventMapping` — domain `eventType` + capability → provider list.
- `IntegrationDeliveryJob` — idempotent work unit `(tenantId, provider, capability, domainEventId)`.

### Workspace `integrationSurface` (Phase B)

Workspaces that support operator-managed integrations declare an optional
`integrationSurface` on `WorkspacePlugin` (workspace-sdk). The API resolves this surface
via `resolveWorkspacePluginForType(workspaceType)` — never by hardcoding Denali/Telegram in
platform core.

Denali is the first implementer (`packages/workspaces/denali/src/integrations/denali-integration.surface.ts`):

| Surface field                                   | Purpose                                                              |
| ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `providers[].id`                                | Provider registry id (`telegram`)                                    |
| `providers[].configFields` / `credentialFields` | Control-plane field contract (`channelId`, `botToken`)               |
| `providers[].defaultCapabilities`               | Connection create default (`message.send`)                           |
| `providers[].defaultEventPolicies`              | Seed `integration_event_policies` on create                          |
| `providers[].eventMappings`                     | Dispatcher maps domain events → capabilities                         |
| `messageTemplates`                              | Worker message text per `eventType` (`{{title}}`, `{{aggregateId}}`) |

Platform core (`apps/api/src/integrations/platform/*`) reads the surface at runtime:

- `integrationMappingsForEvent(eventType, workspaceType)` — no static Denali list
- `seedDefaultEventPoliciesForConnection` — seeds from `defaultEventPolicies`
- `IntegrationPolicyEngine` — empty-policy fallback uses surface defaults (not `if telegram`)
- `process-integration-delivery-once` — formats outbound text from `messageTemplates`

Workspaces without `integrationSurface` enqueue no integration jobs.

`channel.create` remains a generic provider capability but must not appear in workspace
surface mappings until a real channel-creation contract exists.

## Data model

Tables (RLS per tenant, same pattern as `workspace_telegram_bots`):

- `integration_connections` — one row per `(tenant_id, provider, workspace_type)`; `enabled`, `status`, `config`, `secret_ref` (no credentials in API).
- `integration_event_policies` — per-connection event routing (`event_type`, `enabled`).
- `integration_secrets` — internal secret payload keyed by `secret_ref` (never returned via HTTP).
- `integration_delivery_jobs` — async delivery queue with retry metadata.

`WorkspaceTelegramBot` remains until backfill + dual-read removal. Delivery dual-reads legacy table when no `integration_connections` row exists.

## Control plane HTTP

Operator routes (tenant RLS + `requireOperatorSession`):

| Method | Path                                         | Action                                                               |
| ------ | -------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| GET    | `/workspaces/:workspaceId/integrations/meta` | Read workspace integration provider form metadata                    |
| GET    | `/workspaces/:workspaceId/exposure/catalog`  | Read native exposure catalog for field-selection UI                  |
| POST   | `/workspaces/:workspaceId/integrations`      | Create connection + store secret                                     |
| GET    | `/workspaces/:workspaceId/integrations`      | List connections + legacy visibility + delivery summary              |
| GET    | `/integrations/:id`                          | Connection detail (supports `legacy-telegram:{botId}` synthetic ids) |
| PATCH  | `/integrations/:id`                          | Update config / capabilities                                         |
| DELETE | `/integrations/:id`                          | Remove connection + secret                                           |
| POST   | `/integrations/:id/enable`                   | Enable connection (`integration_connections` only)                   |
| POST   | `/integrations/:id/disable`                  | Disable connection (`integration_connections` only)                  |
| POST   | `/integrations/:id/test-connection`          | Provider health check (legacy or new row)                            |
| PATCH  | `/integrations/:id/event-policies/:eventType` | Update event enabled state (routing only)                            |
| PATCH  | `/integrations/:id/exposure-intents/:eventType` | Update native exposure intent for a connection/event                 |

List/detail DTO fields for operator verification (Step 7):

- `backingSource`: `integration_connection` | `legacy_workspace_telegram_bot`
- `legacySourceId`: underlying `workspace_telegram_bots.id` when legacy-backed
- `actionsAllowed`: per-row mutation gates (legacy rows are read-only except `test`)
- `isActiveDeliverySource`: whether dispatcher would route events through this row today
- `fallbackSuppressed`: legacy row visible but inactive because an `integration_connections` telegram row exists
- `summary.activeDeliverySource`: workspace-level active delivery backing

Legacy synthetic connection ids use prefix `legacy-telegram:` so operators can inspect pre-backfill bots without mutating legacy tables from the control plane.

### Create validation and duplicate handling

The Telegram control-plane create path validates provider-owned input before any row is
persisted:

- `provider` must exist in the provider registry.
- The provider must be declared by the workspace plugin `integrationSurface`.
- Required `configFields` and `credentialFields` are read from that surface, trimmed, and
  validated before persistence.
- Denali Telegram currently requires `config.channelId` and credential `botToken`; the token is
  stored only through `secret_ref`.
- Default capabilities come from the provider surface (`["message.send"]` for Denali Telegram).

The database unique scope `(tenant_id, provider, workspace_type)` is mapped to
`INTEGRATION_CONNECTION_ALREADY_EXISTS` (`409`) so the operator UI can guide admins to
manage the existing connection instead of surfacing a generic Prisma conflict.

### Workspace scope resolution (type vs instance)

Integration rows are keyed on tenant + **workspace type** (`integration_connections.workspace_type`, e.g. `denali`), not the workspace **instance** id. The session JWT `workspace_id` claim carries the instance id (e.g. `ws-denali-dev`), and the operator UI calls `/workspaces/:workspaceId/integrations` with the workspace **type** slug.

Therefore workspace authorization must compare the requested workspace type against the tenant's resolved workspace type (`resolveWorkspaceTypeForTenant`) — never raw-equality of the JWT `workspace_id` instance against the path slug. The scope guard accepts a request when the path workspaceId either equals the JWT `workspace_id` instance, or case-insensitively matches the tenant's workspace type; otherwise it returns `INTEGRATION_WORKSPACE_FORBIDDEN`. Detail/enable/disable/test resolve and compare against the tenant workspace type for the same reason. RLS still isolates all rows by `tenant_id`.

## Operator UI (Step 7)

**Denali settings hub:** module `integrations` in `packages/workspaces/denali/src/settings/denali-settings.manifest.ts` (workspace group) → `/settings/integrations`.

Web route proxies control-plane HTTP via Next.js API routes under `/api/workspaces/:workspaceId/integrations` and `/api/integrations/:id/*`.

- **Add provider (admin self-service):** when no `integration_connections` row exists for a
  provider declared in `integrationSurface`, `/settings/integrations` fetches
  `/workspaces/:workspaceId/integrations/meta` and renders `configFields` +
  `credentialFields`. Denali currently renders Telegram `channelId` + `botToken`. Submit calls
  `POST /workspaces/:workspaceId/integrations`; optional “enable after save” calls
  `POST /integrations/:id/enable`. Secrets are stored via `secret_ref` only — never echoed in
  list/detail responses.
- **Edit connection (admin self-service):** when `actionsAllowed.patch` is true for an
  `integration_connection` row, the detail panel renders the same surface-driven fields in edit
  mode. `config` fields (e.g. Telegram `channelId`) are prefilled from the connection DTO; secret
  fields (e.g. `botToken`) render empty — leave blank to keep the stored token, or enter a new
  value to rotate via `PATCH /integrations/:id`. The web BFF proxies PATCH at
  `/api/integrations/:id`. Partial updates are supported: config-only, credential-only, or both in
  one request. Legacy synthetic rows remain read-only (`actionsAllowed.patch === false`).
- List panel shows backing badges, enabled state, and active delivery marker.
- Detail panel shows channel config, secret presence (never token), legacy source id, and fallback suppression warning.
- Enable/disable actions apply only to `integration_connections` rows; legacy rows display read-only guidance pending Step 6 backfill.
- Test connection surfaces `ok`, `code`, `message`, `testedAt`, and `backingSource` inline.

### Test-connection probe message

`POST /integrations/:id/test-connection` performs a **live** provider round-trip: `runProviderTest`
(`apps/api/src/integrations/http/integrations.service.ts`) calls `adapter.sendMessage` with the
synthetic context `domainEventId: "test-connection"`, `eventType: "TestConnection"`. For Telegram
this delivers a real message into the configured `channelId` via
`TelegramProviderAdapter.sendMessage`.

Because the probe lands in the same channel that real subscribers see, the probe text must be
**audience-safe and brand-aligned**, not a raw developer string. The probe body is a workspace-branded
activation confirmation (Denali: a success + branded reassurance line, with a small number of
presentation emojis). Constraints:

- Text is UTF-8 and sent as Telegram plain `text` (no `parse_mode`), so emojis render directly and
  no Markdown/HTML escaping is required.
- The probe carries no tour payload — it must read as a connection/activation notice, never as a
  tour announcement, so it is not driven by `messageTemplates[eventType]` or exposure field
  selection.
- The probe string currently hardcodes the Denali brand name. When a second workspace onboards,
  parametrize it by the workspace display name rather than branching per workspace.

The integrations meta endpoint intentionally describes provider configuration and event routing
only:

- `providers[].defaultEventPolicies` comes from `integrationSurface.providers[].defaultEventPolicies`.
- Field-selection UI reads selectable fields from
  `/workspaces/:workspaceId/exposure/catalog`, not integration meta. During Phase 8 this native
  catalog is still seeded from registry `deliverable` tags until persisted
  [Exposure Profiles](../architecture/field-exposure-system.md#exposureprofile--reusable-defaults)
  land.

This is intentionally read-only metadata for UI rendering. It does not introduce a second event
catalog or a provider-specific field list.

Persisted operator overrides:

- `integration_event_policies.enabled` — whether the connection publishes this event type (routing only).
- `exposure_intents` — explicit native exposure intent (`selectedFieldIds`,
  `templateOverrideId`, mode) scoped by connection + event context.

Admin UI writes field exposure through
`PATCH /integrations/:id/exposure-intents/:eventType` and event routing through
`PATCH /integrations/:id/event-policies/:eventType`. Standalone exposure settings read catalog
data from `/workspaces/:workspaceId/exposure/catalog`; integrations settings may still embed the
generic exposure checklist for connection-scoped convenience.

**No backfill in Step 7** — UI is inspection/control only; `workspace_telegram_bots` rows are not migrated automatically.

## Policy engine

`IntegrationPolicyEngine` evaluates `integration_event_policies` before enqueue:

1. Load enabled connections for tenant + workspace scope.
2. For each connection, check policy row for `(connection_id, event_type)` — `enabled` must be true.
3. If no policy rows exist for a connection, apply provider defaults (seed on create).
4. Dispatcher consults policy engine only — never calls providers.

Each `IntegrationPolicyDecision` carries the connection's native `exposureIntent`
(`ExposureIntent | null`) for that event. Legacy synthetic connections and connections without a
matching intent row resolve to `null` (exposure profile defaults, currently registry-seeded until
Phase 8 persisted profiles land). This lets the dispatcher apply per-connection overrides without
a second repository read.

### Lean delivery field policy (transitional)

> Current pipeline. Target: [Field Exposure evaluation pipeline](../architecture/field-exposure-system.md#evaluation-pipeline). Integrations must not remain the starting point of field selection after Phase 7.

Outbound field selection reuses field policy and event policy contracts during migration:

```text
WorkspacePlugin.integrationSurface.defaultEventPolicies
  -> IntegrationPolicyEngine selects connection + event + capability
  -> native ExposureIntent.selectedFieldIds OR ExposureProfile defaults
  -> filterDeliveryEligibleFields(surface: "<provider ExposureSurface>")
  -> enrichCanonicalDeliveryPayload()
  -> formatIntegrationDeliveryMessage() resolves {{field:<canonicalId>}}
```

For the current Phase 8 path, a workspace can opt in by declaring `fieldPolicy` and using
`{{field:<id>}}` placeholders in `integrationSurface.messageTemplates`. Admin-selected fields
and templates are persisted in `exposure_intents` (not `integration_event_policies`).

`dispatchIntegrationDomainEvent` runs a deterministic per-connection pipeline:

1. Load native `ExposureIntent` for connection + event.
2. `requestedFieldIds` = `intent.selectedFieldIds` when the intent overrides fields, otherwise `ExposureProfile` defaults (registry-seeded until Phase 8 persisted profiles).
3. `filterDeliveryEligibleFields(surface: "<provider ExposureSurface>")` applies FieldPolicy to those candidates.
4. `enrichCanonicalDeliveryPayload()` resolves canonical values.
5. Provider-agnostic job payload is enqueued; formatting and HTTP happen in the worker/provider.

Event policies retain routing (`enabled`) only. Field selection and template overrides are
persisted in `exposure_intents` and exposed to admin UI as `exposureIntents` on the connection
DTO. Do not add new integration-owned field-selection primitives — see
[transitional inventory](../architecture/field-exposure-system.md#transitional-concepts-inventory).

`integrationDeliveryFieldValues` carries enriched eligible values only. A non-null
`ExposureIntent.templateOverrideId` is forwarded to `formatIntegrationDeliveryMessage` as
`integrationDeliveryMessageTemplate`. Profile `defaultTemplateId` mirrors the workspace
surface header seed only and is **not** forwarded as an override — automatic field lines use
`integrationDeliveryFieldIds` when no intent template is set. Provider adapters and workers
must not evaluate field policy; they only receive already-filtered payload metadata or formatted
text.

For events whose outbox payload is intentionally thin (for example `TourCreated` may only carry
`tenantId` and `tourId`), keep existing stable placeholders until the dispatcher enriches canonical
field values. **`TourPublished`** carries a frozen `deliverySnapshot` in the outbox payload; the
dispatcher merges it before field enrichment (see
[integration event catalog](./integration-event-catalog.mdoc) and
[ADR-INT-001](../architecture/adr/ADR-INT-001-tour-published-telegram.mdoc)). A workspace may still
declare `fieldPolicy` first so eligibility metadata is available without changing the delivered
message text.

## Secret handling

- `IntegrationSecretStore` interface — `put` / `get` / `delete` by `secret_ref`.
- Trunk: `PrismaIntegrationSecretStore` → `integration_secrets` table (RLS).
- API DTOs expose `hasSecret: boolean` and opaque `secretRef` prefix only — never raw tokens.
- Control-plane create writes `integration_connections`, `integration_secrets`, and
  `integration_event_policies` in one tenant RLS transaction. A failed secret write rolls the
  connection row back, so operators never see a partially configured provider.
- Secret rotation through PATCH uses the same transaction boundary as the config/capability update.
- Telegram backfill uses the same atomic boundary for migrated connection rows, migrated secrets,
  and default policy seeds.

## Provider plugin contract

Each provider implements:

```typescript
interface IntegrationProviderAdapter {
  readonly id: IntegrationProviderId;
  readonly supportedCapabilities: readonly IntegrationCapability[];
  sendMessage(ctx, input): Promise<IntegrationDeliveryResult>;
  createChannelLink?(ctx, input): Promise<IntegrationDeliveryResult>;
}
```

Register in `integration-provider-registry.ts` at API boot.

## Feature flags

| Env                                   | Default | Meaning                                                                           |
| ------------------------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `INTEGRATION_DELIVERY_ENABLED`        | `false` | Dispatcher enqueues jobs from outbox relay (`true` by dev bootstrap when `DATABASE_URL` set) |
| `INTEGRATION_DELIVERY_WORKER_ENABLED` | `false` | Background worker processes jobs (`true` by dev bootstrap when `DATABASE_URL` set)             |
| `TOUR_PUBLISHED_ROLLOUT_GATE_FATAL`   | `false` | When `true`, integration mutations blocked if denali Telegram lacks `TourPublished` policy (staging) |

Denali Telegram on publish requires all three delivery flags plus [TourPublished rollout](./tour-published-telegram-rollout-plan.mdoc).
| `LEGACY_TELEGRAM_FALLBACK_ENABLED`    | `true`  | Dual-read `workspace_telegram_bots` when no enabled `integration_connections` row |

Set `LEGACY_TELEGRAM_FALLBACK_ENABLED=false` after backfill verification to force `integration_connections` only. Set `true` again to roll back delivery to legacy rows if `integration_connections` rows are removed.

## Security

- Bot tokens stored via `secret_ref` → `integration_secrets` (never in HTTP responses).
- Legacy `integration_connections.credentials` column deprecated; dual-read during migration.
- All outbound URLs pass `assertSafeOutboundUrl` with provider host allowlist (`api.telegram.org`).
- Webhook ingress (future) uses `integration_webhook_events` with HMAC + replay cache (same pattern as payments webhooks).

## Verification

```bash
pnpm --filter @apps/api exec node --import tsx --test \
  src/integrations/platform/integration-provider-registry.spec.ts \
  src/integrations/application/dispatch-integration-domain-event.spec.ts \
  src/integrations/application/integration-policy-engine.spec.ts \
  src/integrations/http/integrations.routes.spec.ts \
  src/integrations/http/integrations-verification.spec.ts \
  src/integrations/migration/telegram-backfill.spec.ts
```

## Migration from WorkspaceTelegramBot (Step 6)

### Backfill script

Idempotent, tenant-scoped, RLS-aware migration from `workspace_telegram_bots` → `integration_connections`.

```bash
# Dry-run (default) — logs intended inserts, no writes
pnpm --filter @apps/api run integrations:backfill-telegram -- --dry-run

# Optional tenant filter
pnpm --filter @apps/api run integrations:backfill-telegram -- --dry-run --tenant=<uuid>

# Apply writes (requires explicit flag)
pnpm --filter @apps/api run integrations:backfill-telegram -- --apply

# Verification only — mismatch report between legacy and new rows
pnpm --filter @apps/api run integrations:verify-telegram-backfill
pnpm --filter @apps/api run integrations:verify-telegram-backfill -- --tenant=<uuid>
```

### Mapping rules

| Legacy (`workspace_telegram_bots`) | Target (`integration_connections`)            |
| ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| `workspace_type`                   | `workspace_type`                              |
| —                                  | `provider = telegram`                         |
| `channel_id`                       | `config.channelId`                            |
| `enabled`                          | `enabled` + `status` (`enabled` / `disabled`) |
| `bot_token`                        | `integration_secrets` via `secret_ref`        |
| `id`                               | `config.migratedFromLegacyBotId` (audit)      |

`secret_ref` values:

- Bot token present: `integration-connection:{connectionId}` with payload in `integration_secrets`.
- No bot token: `migrated-legacy:telegram:{legacyBotId}` marker (no secret payload).

### Safety guarantees

- **Idempotent:** skips when `(tenant_id, provider, workspace_type)` already exists — never overwrites.
- **Tenant-safe:** writes run inside `withTenantRls(tenantId)` per tenant.
- **Retry-safe:** re-run after partial failure only inserts missing rows.
- **Legacy read-only:** `workspace_telegram_bots` rows are never mutated; control plane legacy synthetic ids remain inspection-only.

### Post-backfill delivery preference

`PrismaIntegrationPolicyRepository.listEnabledConnectionsForScope` already prefers `integration_connections` when any enabled row exists. Legacy fallback runs only when the table has no enabled connection **and** `LEGACY_TELEGRAM_FALLBACK_ENABLED=true`.

### Remaining steps

1. ~~Backfill script~~ (this step).
2. Dual-read connections API (done — operator UI).
3. Drop `workspace_telegram_bots` after Architect sign-off.

## System consistency guard

Runtime safety for migration/schema/UI drift is documented in [system-consistency-guard](/dev/system-consistency-guard). API boot runs `runMigrationConsistencyCheck()` and arms or blocks the integration delivery subsystem; operator settings **reads** (settings hub module + `GET` integrations list/meta/detail) stay available during drift, while **writes** return `INTEGRATION_SYSTEM_NOT_READY` until the gate is green.

The guard must include every table required for a healthy integration control plane:
`integration_connections`, `integration_secrets`, `integration_event_policies`,
`integration_delivery_jobs`, and the legacy `workspace_telegram_bots` table during dual-read
migration. Missing `integration_secrets` is treated as subsystem-not-ready because atomic create
depends on that table.
